sections.s2_technical:
The compiler applies the SAME rule to both: the variable must not be reassigned after initialization. Explicitly final enforces this at declaration. Effectively final enforces this by observing that no reassignment occurs anywhere in the scope.

```java
// ═══ EXPLICITLY FINAL — keyword present ═══
final BigDecimal threshold = new BigDecimal("100000");
trades.stream().filter(t -> t.getAmount().compareTo(threshold) > 0);
// threshold is final → cannot be reassigned → lambda captures safely

// ═══ EFFECTIVELY FINAL — no keyword, never reassigned ═══
BigDecimal threshold = new BigDecimal("100000");
// threshold is NEVER reassigned anywhere after this line
trades.stream().filter(t -> t.getAmount().compareTo(threshold) > 0);
// Compiler verifies: threshold not reassigned → effectively final → capture allowed

// ═══ BOTH PRODUCE IDENTICAL BYTECODE ═══
// javap shows the same captured variable mechanism for both
```

If you add a reassignment, the effectively final property is LOST — the lambda no longer compiles:

```java
BigDecimal threshold = new BigDecimal("100000");
threshold = new BigDecimal("200000");  // REASSIGNED! No longer effectively final!
// trades.stream().filter(t -> t.getAmount().compareTo(threshold) > 0);
// COMPILE ERROR: "local variables referenced from a lambda must be final or effectively final"
```

With explicitly `final`, the reassignment itself is a compile error:

```java
final BigDecimal threshold = new BigDecimal("100000");
// threshold = new BigDecimal("200000");  // COMPILE ERROR: "cannot assign a value to final variable"
```

The key insight: `final` catches the reassignment AT THE ASSIGNMENT LINE. Effectively final catches it AT THE LAMBDA LINE. Same protection, different error location.

When to prefer explicit `final`:

Using `final` is preferred when the variable is a configuration value, method parameter, or constant that SHOULD NEVER change. It documents intent and catches reassignment earlier (at the assignment line, not at the lambda). Some teams enforce `final` for all local variables as a coding standard.

```java
// GOOD PRACTICE: final on method parameters
public BigDecimal calculateFee(final Trade trade, final BigDecimal rate) {
    // trade and rate cannot be reassigned — intent is clear
    return trade.getAmount().multiply(rate);
}

// GOOD PRACTICE: final on loop variables when captured
for (final Trade trade : trades) {
    executor.submit(() -> processTrade(trade));  // trade is final — safe capture
}
```

When effectively final is sufficient:

For simple local variables that are obviously never reassigned, `final` adds clutter without value. The compiler guarantees the same safety.

```java
// Effectively final is fine here — obvious, simple:
String accountId = request.getAccountId();
trades.stream().filter(t -> t.getAccountId().equals(accountId));
// No need for: final String accountId = ... (adds noise)
```

